para.p:
A more recent line lifts the treatment from convolutions to attention, with queries, keys, and values that transform predictably under a chosen Lie group. The SE(3)-Transformer [] is the canonical instance, building each attention layer from tensor-field representations so that rotating the input scene rotates the whole stack of outputs consistently; Tensor Field Networks [] use the same machinery without an attention head and have been especially influential in molecular modeling, where SE(3) equivariance is a problem constraint rather than an optional bias. Both parameterize the kernel as a combination of spherical-harmonic basis functions, which is computationally heavy, so a parallel line pursues lighter equivariance: E(
n
)-equivariant graph networks (EGNN) [] achieve E(
n
)-equivariant message passing from relative distances and coordinate updates alone, and Vector Neurons [] lift scalar point-cloud features to 3D vectors, obtaining SO(3) equivariance as a drop-in primitive, each trading representational richness for scalability. With the libraries above, trying equivariance on a new task now costs about as much engineering as a convolutional baseline; the choice of group still has to be made by the designer.

