 [
 ]
introduced OptNet, which treats the solution of a quadratic program as a layer: the forward pass solves

A subclass is tailored to geometric problems that are themselves argmin problems, typically minimizing a reprojection error: camera pose from 2D–3D correspondences, bundle adjustment, inverse kinematics.

                      [
                      ]
                     introduced a differentiable blind PnP solver that estimates 3D pose as a layer; 
                      [
                      ]
                     built a differentiable bundle-adjustment module into a SLAM system, solving for camera poses and 3D points by iterative optimization while letting gradients flow; EPro-PnP [] recasts PnP as a probabilistic layer with a learnable distribution over poses; and Theseus [] provides a reusable library for differentiable nonlinear least squares (Gauss–Newton, Levenberg–Marquardt) that underlies bundle adjustment, pose-graph optimization, and inverse kinematics.
These layers keep the domain-specific optimizer but make it differentiable, so the whole vision or robotics system trains end-to-end while the geometry stays correct and constrained, for instance so that estimated object poses agree across views; 
                      [
                      ]
                     refer to such constructs as declarative nodes and demonstrate them on visual Sudoku and robust geometry estimation.

Beyond continuous optimization, the same construction extends to symbolic reasoning.
SATNet [] integrates a differentiable satisfiability solver as a layer: the forward pass solves a relaxed SAT problem (a quadratic unconstrained binary optimization) to produce assignments consistent with logical constraints, and the backward pass differentiates implicitly through the solver’s fixed-point conditions; 
                      [
                      ]
                     extend differentiable logic programs to abstract visual reasoning benchmarks such as Raven’s Progressive Matrices.
These layers belong to the same class of constraint-based implicit modules as the QP and convex layers, specialized to combinatorial and relational dependencies.

Three routes follow from these axes. Topology-aware sparse interaction (learned edges, hierarchical groupings, or attention masks parameterized by
G t
) preserves long-range coordination while scaling sub-quadratically, with the GNN+PPO patrol architecture of MAGEC [] as an early instantiation and graph encoders combined with sequence-model attention over short temporal windows as the natural next step. The monotonicity ceiling of VDN / QMIX [] can be relaxed by attention-weighted mixers, weighted projection schemes, or dueling-style decompositions that keep individual-global argmax consistency while admitting non-monotonic cooperation, so that COMA-style counterfactual credit assignment [] no longer depends on a joint-action critic that becomes the bottleneck. And explicit objectives for agent attrition and communication disturbance [], paired with failure-centric curricula and hooks for formal verification, move resilience from an emergent property of training to a first-class objective, decisive in safety-critical multi-robot and driving deployments [] where worst-case performance dominates the deployment decision; closed-loop evaluation suites that jointly report team return, safety, calibration, and latency are the missing measurement substrate, and the benchmarks for partner generalization, bandwidth-aware coordination, and sim-to-real transfer that would play the role SMAC and GRF played for CTDE are still under construction.

