para.p:
treating the scene as the implicit 3D structure of a coherent video and the camera trajectory
{
π v
}
as an explicit conditioning channel. Where the feed-forward reconstructors of § output a static asset in one pass, the video paradigm trades static 3D for temporally coherent multi-frame output that mirrors the navigability requirement of a scene more directly. CameraCtrl [] and MotionCtrl [] condition video generation on camera trajectories directly; ViewCrafter [] and Gen3C [] add geometric guidance by unprojecting observed views into an intermediate 3D representation and reprojecting it to the target viewpoints, which improves pose fidelity; and CAT4D [] and 4DiM [] train jointly on posed static multi-view data and on temporal video data, so that viewpoint variation and temporal dynamics are modeled together for 4D scene synthesis.

Evaluation considers geometric fidelity, appearance quality, and semantic alignment with the instruction. Geometry is measured by Chamfer Distance (CD) [], lower being better, and by F-score [], which captures surface overlap at a distance threshold. Texture and appearance are assessed by comparing renderings of the edited representation against target or inpainted images with SSIM [], LPIPS [], and DreamSim [] []. Visual–text alignment is scored with CLIP- or DINO-based embedding similarity [], and GPTEval3D [] uses GPT-4V to compare multi-view renderings of edited shapes on text alignment, 3D plausibility, and texture detail. To measure the edit relative to the input rather than in isolation, directional CLIP (CLIP
dir
) [] checks that the change in image embedding, computed over multi-view renderings of input and output, follows the change in text embedding.

Text-to-video generation is the base case of Eq. () with no auxiliary control (
= κ ∅
): the generator samples a latent from the prompt embedding alone,

Here the condition is a 3D scene, and most methods adopt a multi-stage pipeline that predicts a goal or interaction anchor, plans a trajectory, then generates motion along it. Early works predict a goal from 2D/RGB scene inputs and synthesize motion through trajectory-guided VAEs [], sometimes with scene-aware discriminators. SAMP [] models 3D paths and human–object interactions; later methods refine interaction with per-joint contact maps [] or path-search (
A ∗
) for trajectory diversity [], and GOAL [] and SAGA [] target grasping via two-step goal-to-motion VAEs. Some assume given goals and stitch sub-goal motions hierarchically for long-term synthesis [], while refinement-based frameworks such as CIRCLE [] initialize by interpolation and refine with scene-geometry-aware modules.

A planner must assign trustworthy probabilities to the futures it enumerates, not merely list them: an over-confident missed mode of Eq. () is a safety failure rather than a metric error, so calibrated multimodality is the first open problem. Feasibility and safety are the second: displacement metrics reward trajectories a vehicle cannot physically execute, so feasibility has to be enforced inside the model (
f dyn
) rather than checked afterward, and the long tail of near-collision scenarios is exactly where data are scarcest. The third is closed-loop distribution shift: models trained to imitate logged, open-loop futures drift once their own predictions feed back into the scene, the difficulty ChauffeurNet’s worst-case synthesis was built to expose. Diffusion- and transformer-based scene-level samplers that forecast all agents jointly and run closed-loop are the current direction, and they connect directly to the controllable-video and world-model lines of § and §, in which a driving world model both predicts and is conditioned on the ego plan.

A manipulation system maps raw multimodal observations and a task instruction into executable, constraint-satisfying actions, and this section organizes that mapping into four strategic pivots, each a fundamental shift in how systems bridge perception and physical action.
(i) Grounding Action in Spatial Anchors moves beyond raw pixels to vision priors and sparse spatial tokens (keypoints, dense correspondences) that stay consistent across viewpoints and instances;
(ii) Inferring Functional Action Possibilities shifts from object classification to extracting affordances and tool-use capabilities, bridging perception with contact-rich execution;
(iii) Encoding Spatial Reasoning in Generalist Models transitions from single-task policies to foundation models (VLMs/VLAs) that integrate explicit 3D representations and reasoning traces for cross-embodiment generalization; and
(iv) Satisfying Geometric and Logical Constraints advances from simple trajectory generation to constrained optimization and neuro-symbolic planning (TAMP), ensuring actions respect strict safety limits and long-horizon logical structure.
The four pivots are complementary and mutually reinforcing rather than strictly sequential: anchors and affordances structure the perceptual input, generalist models supply broad priors, and constraint-based planners check feasibility under the modeled geometric, physical, and logical assumptions. Figure  situates them as complementary capabilities within a closed-loop perception-to-action system, and the remainder of this section treats them in this order.
